WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.696 --> 00:00:17.977
the dawn of human history, people have looked up at the stars and longed to reach them.

2
00:00:18.637 --> 00:00:24.299
With the recent advances in mathematics and physics, scientists have been able to invent and build vehicles to

3
00:00:24.339 --> 00:00:27.220
take humans far above Earth and into space.

4
00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:35.671
Science-fiction writers often imagine these people living in space stations—enormous, rotating habitats teeming with activity.

5
00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:43.138
In reality, a program began in the 1980s to build a simpler, more basic facility that has become

6
00:00:43.178 --> 00:00:45.240
known as the International Space Station.

7
00:00:46.361 --> 00:00:52.026
A space station is a structure orbiting Earth that human beings can inhabit for extended periods of time.

8
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:58.987
Since 1971, nine space stations have been launched into orbit and occupied by astronauts.

9
00:00:59.948 --> 00:01:04.412
Some of these stations have been built by the Soviet Union and others by the United States.

10
00:01:05.092 --> 00:01:10.697
The International Space Station is unique in that it is the result of cooperation between many countries.

11
00:01:11.998 --> 00:01:14.520
During the height of the cold war in 1961, U.S.

12
00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:16.301
President John F.

13
00:01:16.321 --> 00:01:19.484
Kennedy vowed to put a man on the moon before the decade was over.

14
00:01:20.172 --> 00:01:20.572
The U.S.

15
00:01:20.592 --> 00:01:24.775
succeeded in sending astronauts to the moon on July 20, 1969.

16
00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:31.839
But the financial burden of putting astronauts on the moon meant that the National Aeronautics and Space Administration

17
00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:35.061
(NASA) had to postpone some of its plans.

18
00:01:36.282 --> 00:01:42.606
It wasn't until after the cold war had ended, in 1991, that plans for the International Space Station

19
00:01:42.626 --> 00:01:43.847
began to materialize.

20
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:46.476
In 1993, U.S.

21
00:01:46.536 --> 00:01:50.719
President Bill Clinton urged NASA to join forces with its Russian counterpart.

22
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.063
One of the trickiest parts of combining the efforts of these two former rivals was learning how to

23
00:01:56.123 --> 00:02:02.408
work together after having had separate space programs and not having shared knowledge for the previous thirty years.

24
00:02:03.328 --> 00:02:08.852
The Russians had succeeded in sending up and occupying several space stations for varying lengths of time.

25
00:02:10.053 --> 00:02:15.937
The U.S., on the other hand, had concentrated on developing the space shuttle, an airplane-like vehicle that could

26
00:02:15.957 --> 00:02:19.219
be used multiple times to carry passengers into space and back.

27
00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:25.902
The integration of the two nations' space programs began when NASA's space shuttles began to ferry American astronauts

28
00:02:25.942 --> 00:02:30.525
to the already orbiting Russian space station, Mir, in 1995.

29
00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:37.328
This allowed American astronauts to learn the practical know-how of operating and occupying a space station.

30
00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:43.792
Russian astronauts, on the other hand, benefited because they were finally able to complete the structure of Mir

31
00:02:44.236 --> 00:02:46.217
with the funding they received from the U.S.

32
00:02:47.298 --> 00:02:53.081
In November 1998, the first module of the International Space Station was launched on top of a Russian

33
00:02:53.121 --> 00:03:01.866
rocket. Due to growing international cooperation, this component, called Zarya, which means "sunrise" in Russian, had been built

34
00:03:01.966 --> 00:03:04.388
in Russia using American funding and technology.

35
00:03:05.628 --> 00:03:11.251
A little bigger than a standard bus, Zarya was designed to provide electricity and propulsion for the International

36
00:03:11.311 --> 00:03:15.230
Space Station, but it did not include living quarters for crew members.

37
00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:20.814
Next, the American space shuttle Endeavour met up with Zarya and attached the U.S.

38
00:03:20.854 --> 00:03:22.235
connection module Unity.

39
00:03:23.216 --> 00:03:26.719
Finally, another Russian module was attached to the ISS.

40
00:03:27.119 --> 00:03:32.022
Called Zvezda, meaning "star," this structure was the crew living area.

41
00:03:33.023 --> 00:03:39.448
In October 2000, Expedition One, the first ISS crew, consisting of two Russians and one American, arrived.

42
00:03:40.456 --> 00:03:46.681
Expedition Two included a woman astronaut, and subsequent expeditions have included crew members from countries such as Spain

43
00:03:46.781 --> 00:03:47.322
and Brazil.

44
00:03:48.162 --> 00:03:54.127
The International Space Station is the product of the cooperation of nearly twenty space agencies around the world.

45
00:03:55.408 --> 00:04:02.073
In 2001, the space shuttle Atlantis delivered a laboratory called Destiny to the International Space Station to study

46
00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:03.395
the effects of zero gravity.

47
00:04:04.475 --> 00:04:10.488
Destiny was a monumental step for the ISS because it had the facilities to conduct scientific experiments that

48
00:04:10.508 --> 00:04:13.991
could lead to a deeper understanding of the effects of long-term space travel.

49
00:04:14.852 --> 00:04:19.395
Much of the research being done in the ISS has to do with zero gravity and its effects

50
00:04:19.455 --> 00:04:20.236
on the human body.

51
00:04:21.237 --> 00:04:27.161
Zero gravity, or the weightlessness that humans experience in outer space, feels like constantly falling from a great

52
00:04:27.221 --> 00:04:29.003
height or riding a roller coaster.

53
00:04:29.964 --> 00:04:35.448
Scientists have discovered that in a weightless environment, bodily fluids tend to stay in the upper body.

54
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.921
Many astronauts have shown a decrease in strength in their lower body as well as a significant loss

55
00:04:41.041 --> 00:04:43.722
of bone calcium after long periods of weightlessness.

56
00:04:45.043 --> 00:04:50.344
The number of red blood cells in the bloodstream, those that deliver oxygen to the body's tissues, also

57
00:04:50.404 --> 00:04:53.345
goes down, which can lead to a variety of health problems.

58
00:04:54.125 --> 00:05:00.187
When astronauts began to do certain exercises on specially designed machines while living in the ISS, the negative

59
00:05:00.227 --> 00:05:02.748
effects of zero gravity on their bodies were reduced.

60
00:05:03.856 --> 00:05:10.001
As scientists study how weightlessness affects the human body, they are also conducting many other experiments.

61
00:05:10.961 --> 00:05:17.306
By studying how proteins, viruses, and living cells behave in space, scientists may be able to develop more

62
00:05:17.346 --> 00:05:18.266
effective medicines.

63
00:05:19.227 --> 00:05:23.990
Observing how fluids and flames behave differently when they're not subject to Earth's gravity can lead to new

64
00:05:24.151 --> 00:05:26.893
alloys and other materials for industrial use.

65
00:05:27.973 --> 00:05:35.059
Last but not least, watching Earth from space allows scientists to document the effects of environmental damage, volcanic

66
00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:37.741
eruptions, pollution, and many other factors.

67
00:05:39.022 --> 00:05:45.368
Since the addition of Destiny, several European countries, as well as the Japanese space agency, have contributed other

68
00:05:45.408 --> 00:05:46.789
components to the ISS.

69
00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:54.235
It now has docking compartments, robotic arms, solar panels, and an array of other gadgets that allow astronauts

70
00:05:54.255 --> 00:05:59.720
to perform safer space walks, and of course, conduct more scientific experiments.

71
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:06.023
The International Space Station also has a "lifeboat" in the form of a Russian Soyuz spacecraft that could

72
00:06:06.083 --> 00:06:08.644
return the crew to Earth in the case of an emergency.

73
00:06:09.365 --> 00:06:11.406
Since 1998, most of the U.S.

74
00:06:11.446 --> 00:06:14.688
space shuttles' flights have been used to work on the ISS.

75
00:06:15.728 --> 00:06:18.410
The progress of the space station has come at a price, however.

76
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:25.754
Sadly, in 2003, the space shuttle Columbia disintegrated as it was returning from orbit, and all seven crew

77
00:06:25.774 --> 00:06:26.974
members aboard died.

78
00:06:28.375 --> 00:06:35.713
The shuttle fleet was grounded, which left the International Space Station's development virtually at a standstill until 2005.

79
00:06:36.614 --> 00:06:41.658
Since then, construction has picked up but proceeds more slowly than before the Columbia disaster.

80
00:06:42.499 --> 00:06:47.523
There were only four space shuttle flights to the ISS between 2005 and June 2007.

81
00:06:48.484 --> 00:06:54.589
Even at this reduced pace, however, the ISS is expected to be mostly completed by 2010.

82
00:06:55.510 --> 00:07:00.265
Although the development of the ISS has slowed down, there has been some growth in the area of

83
00:07:00.325 --> 00:07:01.085
space tourism.

84
00:07:01.846 --> 00:07:07.889
A man named Denis Tito made world history on April 28, 2001, when he became the first paying

85
00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:12.772
passenger to travel to the International Space Station for the price of twenty million dollars.

86
00:07:14.253 --> 00:07:19.496
Some argue that while there are still so many problems on Earth, we should not be using valuable

87
00:07:19.556 --> 00:07:21.617
resources for space exploration.

88
00:07:22.537 --> 00:07:27.820
While they may have a valid point that the budget set aside for space exploration and research could

89
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:35.004
be used to fight crime, reduce poverty, and improve social welfare systems, space exploration has improved the quality

90
00:07:35.044 --> 00:07:35.745
of our lives.

91
00:07:36.625 --> 00:07:44.029
Many of the technologies originally developed for the space program have practical applications on Earth, such as water-purification

92
00:07:44.069 --> 00:07:47.271
treatments and scratch-resistant lenses for eyeglasses.

93
00:07:48.171 --> 00:07:52.314
Perhaps in the future, humans will be able to travel to the far corners of the galaxy.

94
00:07:53.294 --> 00:07:59.833
Today, however, they can hope for a better future in seeing that because of international cooperation, a project

95
00:07:59.873 --> 00:08:04.800
that was not feasible for a single nation to manage has been accomplished by many working together.
